From c19b3b79254da3bbffe3c6260b51d627c8af26a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 20:44:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] fix(api): the caller no longer chooses whose data they
 read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Deletes every `tenant_id` query parameter as a source of truth. Tenancy comes
from the session, and a tenant_id in a request is either ignored or refused —
never obeyed.

  lib/apiAuth.ts    resolves the Clerk subject to a users row, then to a tenant
                    and a role. Fails closed: 503 with no identity provider
                    configured, 401 for a session with no users row, 403 for a
                    real operator reaching outside their tenant or above their
                    role. resolveTenantScope() is the function that replaces
                    searchParams.get("tenant_id").
  lib/db/server.ts  replaces lib/db/client.ts. Every tenant-scoped query takes
                    tenantId as a required parameter, so forgetting to scope a
                    request is a type error rather than a data breach.

lib/db/client.ts is deleted, not deprecated. It connected with the anon key and
was imported by exactly four files, all API routes — no browser ever used it.
Against the migrated schema it could not complete a query in either direction,
and it fell back to https://example.supabase.co when unconfigured, so a
completely non-functional data layer looked healthy apart from one console
warning at boot.

The sharpest fix is updateJob. It was updateJob(id, updates) with no tenant
filter at all: a known job id from any caller was writable in any tenant. It is
now updateJob(id, tenantId, updates), and the .eq("tenant_id") is load-bearing.

Platform staff (system_admin, support) may still name a tenant, because
supporting a pilot means being able to look at it — and must name one, since an
implicit "all tenants" is how a support tool becomes an export of the whole
platform. pilot_admin is deliberately not a platform role: it administers a
flooring company's account, and cross-tenant reach there would let any pilot
customer read every other pilot's jobs.

Two more dead validators found and wired up. PATCH /api/applications/[id]
assigned status straight from the body with no enum check, exactly as the jobs
route did, while validatePilotApplicationUpdate sat unused at
lib/validators.ts:105. And the Clerk import moved behind an injected
SessionReader with a dynamic-import default — an authorization boundary that
can only run inside a live Next.js server with a live Clerk session is one
nobody tests.

